latest_plan = 'Based on the reflection, here\'s a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Create a deep copy of the input grid to work on.\n\n2. Define a function to determine if two cells are part of the same "logical" shape:\n   - This function should return true if the cells are either directly adjacent (including diagonally) or within a certain distance (e.g., 2 or 3 cells apart).\n\n3. Identify all blue cells in the grid:\n   - Iterate through the grid and store the coordinates of all blue (8) cells in a set.\n\n4. Group blue cells into logical shapes:\n   - Start with any blue cell and use a breadth-first search (BFS) to find all cells that are part of the same logical shape.\n   - Use the function from step 2 to determine connectivity.\n   - Store each shape as a set of coordinates.\n   - Remove the cells of each identified shape from the set of blue cells.\n   - Repeat until all blue cells have been assigned to a shape.\n\n5. For each identified shape:\n   - Find the bounding box of the shape (min and max x and y coordinates).\n   - Expand this bounding box by 1 cell in each direction to create the outline area.\n\n6. Create the outline for each shape:\n   - Iterate through the cells in the expanded bounding box.\n   - If a cell is not blue and is adjacent (including diagonally) to any blue cell in the shape, mark it as part of the outline.\n   - However, don\'t mark a cell as outline if it would overwrite another blue cell or a non-black cell.\n\n7. Apply the outlines to the grid:\n   - For each cell marked as outline in step 6, set its color to red (2) in the grid copy.\n\n8. Return the modified grid.\n\nAdditional considerations:\n\n- In step 2, the distance threshold for considering cells part of the same shape might need to be adjusted based on further examples or requirements. Start with a distance of 2 and adjust if necessary.\n\n- In step 6, when checking adjacency to blue cells, consider not just immediate adjacency but also cells within the distance threshold used in step 2. This ensures that isolated blue cells that are part of the shape are properly outlined.\n\n- Implement helper functions as needed, such as a function to get all cells within the distance threshold of a given cell, to make the main algorithm cleaner and more modular.\n\n- Throughout the process, ensure that the original blue cells are never modified.\n\nThis plan addresses the nuanced definition of shapes we identified in the reflection, handling both connected and nearby-but-disconnected blue cells as part of the same logical shape. It also ensures that outlines don\'t overwrite existing blue or non-black cells, and that each logical shape gets a single, unified outline.'
